      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $(x-2)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+7x-6,$ factor $f(x)$ over the real numbers.",
      "worked_solution": "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+7x-6}{x-2}={x}^{2}-2x+3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+7x-6=(x-2)({x}^{2}-2x+3).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-2x+3$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-2x+3$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+7x-6=(x-2)({x}^{2}-2x+3).\n$$",
